/**
 * CLOUDFLARE WORKERS DEPLOYMENT.
 *
 * This site is NOT static and cannot be deployed as one. It has API
 * routes, server components, server-side pricing calculation that must
 * never reach the browser, admin authentication, and a service-role
 * Supabase key that only exists server-side. It runs as a Worker, via
 * the OpenNext adapter, which is Cloudflare's supported path for a
 * full Next.js application.
 *
 * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT.
 *
 * No R2 incremental-cache binding. The adapter's docs recommend R2 for
 * a site that uses `revalidate` (this one does: 60 on the root layout,
 * which is also the effective window for /cases), but R2 is not enabled
 * on the account and enabling it is a billing decision that belongs to
 * the owner. The incremental cache is KV instead — see the
 * kv_namespaces block below and open-next.config.ts for why, and for
 * the 500 its absence once caused. Same billing reasoning for the
 * Images binding: without it /_next/image passes files through
 * unresized (docs/CLOUDFLARE_DEPLOYMENT.md).
 *
 * No `run_worker_first` either — see the comment in the assets block:
 * public/_headers now carries the static-file headers it was added for.
 */
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "dockentra-website",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",

  /**
   * 2025-05-05 or later is required by the adapter: earlier dates hit
   * a FinalizationRegistry error in the Workers runtime.
   */
  "compatibility_date": "2025-05-05",

  /**
   * nodejs_compat: the app uses node:crypto (timing-safe comparison of
   * the admin token, HMAC verification of the WhatsApp webhook) and
   * node:path. global_fetch_strictly_public is required by the adapter
   * so that fetches to our own public hostname go out through the
   * internet rather than looping back inside the Worker.
   */
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],

  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS",

    /**
     * No `run_worker_first`. It was here to force /media through the
     * Worker so next.config.ts's cache-control rule would apply, but
     * public/_headers sets that rule directly on the asset response —
     * which is the documented mechanism, costs no Worker invocation
     * per image, and covers the security headers at the same time.
     *
     * Those security headers are the reason public/_headers exists at
     * all: Workers Assets serves a matching static file BEFORE the
     * Worker runs, so nothing under /public was receiving the headers
     * next.config.ts sets. Measured, not assumed — an HTML route came
     * back with 6 and /brand/*.png with 0.
     */
    "not_found_handling": "none"
  },

  /**
   * The Next incremental cache. NEXT_INC_CACHE_KV is the binding name
   * the adapter looks for — it is not free-form. See open-next.config.ts
   * for why this is KV rather than R2 or the read-only assets cache,
   * and for the 500 that its absence caused.
   */
  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "NEXT_INC_CACHE_KV",
      "id": "462a119c87504dd79773fb2fedd21edb"
    }
  ],

  /**
   * A binding to ITSELF, so the site can revalidate its own stale
   * pages. See open-next.config.ts for the "Failed to revalidate
   * stale page" error this fixes — MemoryQueue reads this exact
   * binding name (env.WORKER_SELF_REFERENCE) to make the internal HEAD
   * request that actually regenerates a page past its revalidate
   * window. `service` must equal `name` above; if the Worker is ever
   * renamed both need to change together.
   */
  "services": [
    { "binding": "WORKER_SELF_REFERENCE", "service": "dockentra-website" }
  ],

  /**
   * NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL IS NEEDED AT RUNTIME, NOT ONLY AT BUILD TIME,
   * AND THE FIRST PRODUCTION DEPLOY PROVED IT THE HARD WAY.
   *
   * The build was run with this value set, and the SITEMAP and
   * ROBOTS.TXT came out correct — they are generated once, at build
   * time. Every page, however, is rendered per request, and
   * resolveSiteUrl() reads process.env at that moment. With the value
   * absent from the Worker's runtime environment it fell through to
   * its localhost fallback, and the live site served:
   *
   *   <link rel="canonical" href="http://localhost:3000/pricing">
   *   <meta property="og:url" content="http://localhost:3000">
   *
   * on every single page, while the sitemap alongside them said
   * dockentra.ie. Nothing errored. Google would have been told that
   * the canonical version of every page is a machine on nobody's
   * network.
   *
   * It lives here rather than in the dashboard so it is version
   * controlled and cannot be lost: it is a public URL, not a secret.
   * Secrets stay in `wrangler secret put` and never appear in this file.
   */
  /**
   * PRICING_EMAIL_DELIVERY_MODE / PRICING_EMAIL_FROM — NEITHER WAS SET,
   * AND BOTH ARE REQUIRED FOR "GET PRICE" AND CONTACT TO EMAIL ANYONE.
   *
   * Confirmed empirically against live production before this change:
   * a real /api/pricing/email submission was saved correctly and
   * returned `"delivery": "unavailable"` — resolvePricingEmailDeliveryMode()
   * in src/lib/email/provider.ts falls back to "disabled" with neither
   * var present, regardless of RESEND_API_KEY already being configured
   * as a secret. The OWNER notification for both the pricing calculator
   * AND the three lead forms (Contact / Become a Client / Partnerships
   * — see src/lib/leads/notify.ts) reads this exact same gate, so its
   * absence silenced every one of them at once.
   *
   * PRICING_EMAIL_FROM must be on a domain VERIFIED with Resend — see
   * src/lib/email/provider.ts's isUnverifiableSender(), which refuses
   * a free-mail sender outright. notifications@dockentra.ie is used
   * here because the production domain is what a visitor would
   * recognise; if it turns out dockentra.ie is not yet added and
   * verified in the Resend account, every send attempt fails CLOSED
   * (the lead stays saved, the visitor is told truthfully that
   * delivery isn't available, nothing is ever reported falsely) and
   * Resend's own rejection reason is logged as a short error code —
   * see docs/CLOUDFLARE_DEPLOYMENT.md for how to confirm and fix that.
   *
   * Not secrets: a delivery mode and a sending address are
   * configuration, not credentials. RESEND_API_KEY is the credential,
   * and it stays a `wrangler secret` and never appears here.
   */
  "vars": {
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL": "https://dockentra.ie",
    "PRICING_EMAIL_DELIVERY_MODE": "resend",
    "PRICING_EMAIL_FROM": "notifications@dockentra.ie"
  },

  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
